  { q: 'Did the Norse sacrifice ravens to Odin?', a: 'Direct textual evidence for raven sacrifice as a specific ritual act in Norse religion is limited, but the raven\'s status as Odin\'s sacred bird makes it plausible. What is documented is the general practice of blót — animal sacrifice to the gods — with cattle, horses, pigs, and other animals. The raven\'s privileged status (as extensions of Odin\'s mind) may have made them too sacred for ordinary sacrifice, or sacrifice of ravens may have occurred but not been specifically recorded in surviving sources. Archaeological evidence for corvid remains in ritual contexts exists from several Viking Age sites.' },
  { q: 'What is a battle-raven in Norse culture?', a: 'The battle-raven (Old Norse: valravn or hrafn) is a figure from Norse poetry and belief combining the raven with the battlefield dead. Ravens fed on battlefield corpses, and this real-world behaviour was interpreted as the birds consuming the warrior\'s knowledge and soul. The valravn — a raven that has eaten the heart of a fallen king — was believed to acquire human intelligence and transformative powers. This intersection of raven, warrior death, and power represents a distinct strand of Norse raven symbolism beyond the Huginn-and-Muninn mythology.' },
